WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.389 --> 00:00:09.210
According to Forbes – last time they published
their rich list – Amazon owner Jeff Bezos

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:15.389
was the richest man in the world, with a fortune
estimated at around 177 billion dollars.

00:00:15.389 --> 00:00:20.560
That’s a number that’s quite hard to get
your head around, so let me put it this way:

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:30.340
Jeff Bezos’ personal fortune is larger than
the GDP of 75% of countries around the world.

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:37.750
That’s one man wielding the equivalent riches
of a nation, and a fairly big one at that.

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:44.200
This is a level of wealth nobody could conceivably
spend in a lifetime, no matter how expensive

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:45.250
their tastes.

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:49.660
Seriously - Jeff is 57 years of age at the
time of recording this video.

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:55.309
Now, he’s probably going to have his head
cryogenically frozen and live forever, but

00:00:55.309 --> 00:00:59.960
for the sake of making a point, let’s assume
he’s going to live to 80.

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:07.530
In order to use up all of his 177 billion
dollars before he died (discounting interest),

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:15.350
he would need to spend almost 22 million dollars
every single day for the rest of his life.

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:23.049
That’s well over 10 times the average income
an American makes over their entire career,

00:01:23.049 --> 00:01:28.670
every single day, for about 8400 days in a
row.

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:29.670
Yeeeeah.

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:32.490
But here’s something interesting.

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:38.130
While Jeff Bezos may well be the richest man
in modern times, he isn’t the richest human

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:40.860
being ever to have lived.

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:47.869
In fact, remarkably enough, if you adjust
for inflation, he isn’t even close.

00:01:47.869 --> 00:01:54.340
The individual who holds that title is estimated
to have been in possession of a fortune well

00:01:54.340 --> 00:02:01.420
over twice the size of poor old Jeff’s,
and larger than the fortunes of Elon Musk,

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:06.859
Mark Zuckerberg, Warren Buffet, and Jack Ma…
combined.

00:02:06.859 --> 00:02:13.370
The funny thing is, despite his vaunted financial
position in the annals of human history, there’s

00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:17.590
a very good chance you’ve never even heard
of this guy.

00:02:17.590 --> 00:02:26.150
That being the case, let me introduce you:
This is Mansa Musa - the richest man in history.

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:31.840
As for why you haven’t heard of him, there
are two good reasons for that - first, he

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:34.930
was born almost 750 years ago.

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:38.700
And second, he was the sultan of the Mali
empire.

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:43.659
Most of my viewers live in Europe and the
US - two parts of the world whose school systems

00:02:43.659 --> 00:02:47.280
have not traditionally put a lot of emphasis
on African history.

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:52.330
But that’s a shame, because the Mali empire
- the largest ever seen in Western Africa

00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:54.890
- was really quite something.

00:02:54.890 --> 00:03:00.690
At its height in the 14th century, it covered
almost half a million square miles and was

00:03:00.690 --> 00:03:04.680
home to around 5% of the world’s population.

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:09.680
It rose incredibly quickly - in the space
of just a couple of generations during what’s

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:13.290
known as the golden age of trade in Africa.

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:18.890
The rise of the Mali empire was built on its
unfathomable wealth, and that came primarily

00:03:18.890 --> 00:03:25.970
from two things: gold, and salt, both of which
were in rich local supply.

00:03:25.970 --> 00:03:33.099
So much so that at the time, approximately
half of all the gold in the Old World - that’s

00:03:33.099 --> 00:03:38.940
Europe, Africa, and Asia - came from just
3 goldmines in Mali.

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:45.430
The idea that a vast fortune could come from
gold is no surprise, but the fact salt was

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:50.640
such a major factor might seem a little odd
when viewed through our modern lens.

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:57.099
But in many parts of fourteenth century Africa,
salt was an incredibly valuable commodity

00:03:57.099 --> 00:04:02.440
- to the right buyer, it was literally worth
its weight in gold.

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:08.129
As unlikely as that may sound, it actually
makes a lot of sense when you think about

00:04:08.129 --> 00:04:09.129
it.

00:04:09.129 --> 00:04:14.849
After all, salt is an essential part of the
human diet - we quite literally can’t survive

00:04:14.849 --> 00:04:15.849
without it.

00:04:15.849 --> 00:04:21.290
It also happens to make bland food much more
palatable, and can be used as a preservative,

00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:26.700
which was especially useful before the invention
of reliable refrigeration.

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:31.020
In regions where salt was sparse, it was priceless.

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:35.520
Gold, on the other hand, is… shiny.

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:40.770
We use it in all sorts of things today - from
electronics to spaceships - but the fact it

00:04:40.770 --> 00:04:46.570
looked kinda pretty was about the extent of
its appeal in the 14th century.

00:04:46.570 --> 00:04:52.700
Mansa Musa was born sometime around 1280 AD,
and he came to the throne as the 10th ruler

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:57.110
of the Mali empire in approximately 1312 AD.

00:04:57.110 --> 00:05:03.130
His rise to power was a slightly odd one - he
was only ever supposed to be a temporary ruler,

00:05:03.130 --> 00:05:09.760
selected for this caretaker position when
his predecessor, Abubakari Keita II, decided

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:13.180
to go and find out what was on the other side
of the Atlantic Ocean.

00:05:13.180 --> 00:05:14.480
As you do.

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:21.590
The emperor took some 3000 ships and set off
into the vast expanse of ocean, leaving behind

00:05:21.590 --> 00:05:27.650
his throne and one of the greatest fortunes
in history, all without a shred of evidence

00:05:27.650 --> 00:05:31.380
that there was anything to actually discover
on the other side.

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:39.250
So far as we know, neither Abubakari Keita
II nor his 3000-strong fleet was never seen

00:05:39.250 --> 00:05:46.810
again, though a few historians believe he
did indeed make it all the way to South America…

00:05:46.810 --> 00:05:49.690
but that’s a story for another day.

00:05:49.690 --> 00:05:55.840
The important bit for our purposes is that
this one-time ruler of the Mali empire never

00:05:55.840 --> 00:06:03.480
came back from his bold voyage, and so Mansa
Musa officially ascended to the throne, where

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:10.490
he set about extending the borders of his
lands and growing his already vast wealth.

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:16.150
By the way, I should point out that ‘Mansa’
is a job title rather than a first name - it

00:06:16.150 --> 00:06:18.660
means ‘emperor’ or ‘sultan’.

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:26.010
Anyway, as the head of one of Africa’s greatest
empires, just how rich was Mansa Musa?

00:06:26.010 --> 00:06:29.330
Well before we get to the numbers, how about
an example.

00:06:29.330 --> 00:06:35.230
The new emperor was a devout Muslim, meaning
much like today, it was his solemn duty to

00:06:35.230 --> 00:06:41.300
undertake the Hajj at least once in his lifetime
(the Hajj being a pilgrimage to Mecca and

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:43.600
one of the 5 pillars of Islam).

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:50.590
It’s no exaggeration to say that, when Mansa
Musa decided it was time to go to Mecca, the

00:06:50.590 --> 00:06:57.130
pilgrimage that ensued from Mali to the Arabian
peninsula is one of the most remarkable journeys

00:06:57.130 --> 00:06:59.080
ever made by a human being.

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:01.890
Well, I say ‘a’ human being.

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:09.690
What I actually mean is, 60,000 men and women,
100 elephants, 80 camels, a personal guard

00:07:09.690 --> 00:07:17.580
of 500 highly trained warriors, and enough
food, water, and animals to eat for everyone.

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:24.840
Basically, Mansa Musa undertook this 2700
miles pilgrimage at the centre of an entire

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:30.340
movable city - a caravan that stretched longer
than the eye could see.

00:07:30.340 --> 00:07:35.920
I mean, it was bigger and more extravagant
even than the entourage Aladdin took with

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:40.220
him to impress Princess Jasmine, and that
was made by a magic genie.

00:07:40.220 --> 00:07:44.890
Now, if you’re anything like me you’ll
like to take a bit of spending money when

00:07:44.890 --> 00:07:50.860
you go on a trip, and it seems Mansa Musa
did too, because tucked away in a bum bag

00:07:50.860 --> 00:07:56.980
- I absolutely refuse to say fanny pack - he’d
reserved a little extra cash to treat himself

00:07:56.980 --> 00:07:57.980
along the way.

00:07:57.980 --> 00:08:04.200
Of course, ‘a little extra cash’ has something
of a different meaning when you’re the richest

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:10.460
man ever - in this case, it was 21,000 kilograms
of solid gold.

00:08:10.460 --> 00:08:15.090
You know, just in case there were some bargains
to be had in duty free.

00:08:15.090 --> 00:08:21.280
You might be wondering how exactly he transported
such a vast amount of gold – or at least,

00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:26.860
the twenty thousand nine hundred and ninety-nine
kilograms that didn’t fit in his bum bag.

00:08:26.860 --> 00:08:34.030
That job was left to his 12,000 slaves, each
of whom was given almost 2 kilos in solid

00:08:34.030 --> 00:08:35.979
gold apiece.

00:08:35.979 --> 00:08:42.540
Along with the bullion, Mansa Musa also brought
thousands of kilos of gold dust, which was

00:08:42.540 --> 00:08:45.490
used as currency throughout his kingdom.

00:08:45.490 --> 00:08:51.810
Take all this together - the tens of thousands
of people and hundreds of animals, the unimaginable

00:08:51.810 --> 00:08:57.490
quantities of gold - and it’s fair to say
the world had never before seen anything quite

00:08:57.490 --> 00:09:02.960
like the display of wealth Mansa Musa put
on during his trip to Mecca.

00:09:02.960 --> 00:09:09.210
And yet, in reality, this extravagant show
of riches wasn’t all that it seemed.

00:09:09.210 --> 00:09:15.150
As you may well know, another of the pillars
of Islam is Zakat - which is the giving alms

00:09:15.150 --> 00:09:16.600
to charitable causes.

00:09:16.600 --> 00:09:22.280
That’s right, Mansa Musa hadn’t brought
all this gold for himself - he was planning

00:09:22.280 --> 00:09:23.970
to give it away.

00:09:23.970 --> 00:09:25.340
Like, all of it.

00:09:25.340 --> 00:09:32.050
It’s said that so much gold was handed out
to the poor en-route to Mecca that Mansa Musa

00:09:32.050 --> 00:09:38.140
singlehandedly crashed the price of the commodity
across most of North Africa and the western

00:09:38.140 --> 00:09:46.660
Arabian peninsula, including in major gold-trading
cities like Cairo, Medina, and Mecca itself,

00:09:46.660 --> 00:09:50.310
precipitating a decade long recession.

00:09:50.310 --> 00:09:53.170
He was basically the Mr. Beast of his day.

00:09:53.170 --> 00:09:58.060
But his generosity was about more than just
cold hard gold dust.

00:09:58.060 --> 00:10:01.820
Mansa Musa also wanted to share his beliefs
far and wide.

00:10:01.820 --> 00:10:07.820
But being the richest man in history, he didn’t
settle for simply spreading the word.

00:10:07.820 --> 00:10:14.730
Instead, he started a tradition - every Friday
for the entirety of the pilgrimage, no matter

00:10:14.730 --> 00:10:19.160
where he found himself, he had a mosque built.

00:10:19.160 --> 00:10:25.080
Considering the trip took almost 2 years in
total, that’s a lot of mosques.

00:10:25.080 --> 00:10:29.230
In the interests of presenting a balanced
view, I should point out that a lot of time

00:10:29.230 --> 00:10:34.800
has passed since Mansa Musa ruled over the
Mali empire, and written records from the

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:37.720
time are pretty much non-existent.

00:10:37.720 --> 00:10:42.350
That means it’s difficult to be completely
sure his intentions on this pilgrimage were

00:10:42.350 --> 00:10:45.430
solely philanthropic and spiritual.

00:10:45.430 --> 00:10:52.130
Some historians suspect he was keen to show
off his immense wealth and resources as a

00:10:52.130 --> 00:10:59.820
kind of medieval PR exercise - a branding
mission designed to impress his friends, frighten

00:10:59.820 --> 00:11:04.870
his enemies, and just generally put the Mali
empire on the map.

00:11:04.870 --> 00:11:11.720
And it certainly did exactly that - both figuratively
and literally.

00:11:11.720 --> 00:11:16.760
Because as a direct result of the fame Mansa
Musa gained for himself and his empire on

00:11:16.760 --> 00:11:23.130
his way to Mecca, both would soon begin to
crop up on world maps for the first time,

00:11:23.130 --> 00:11:28.670
including the Catalan Atlas - one of the most
important medieval maps of the 14th century.

00:11:28.670 --> 00:11:33.560
You can see Mansa Musa himself right here,
holding a gold coin and just generally looking

00:11:33.560 --> 00:11:35.440
like a bit of a boss.

00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:41.360
The pilgrimage also acted like a sort of epic
recruitment campaign, with Mansa Musa’s

00:11:41.360 --> 00:11:47.910
wealth, generosity, and effortless power winning
him thousands of supporters and securing his

00:11:47.910 --> 00:11:54.300
empire the services of some of the most celebrated
thinkers and artists of the era - many of

00:11:54.300 --> 00:12:00.180
whom he brought back west when the caravan
returned home after completing the Hajj.

00:12:00.180 --> 00:12:04.860
Speaking of returning home, it was around
this time that Mansa Musa began to sprinkle

00:12:04.860 --> 00:12:11.060
a little gold dust within the borders of his
own lands, too, building mosques and madrasas

00:12:11.060 --> 00:12:15.870
- those are educational institutions - in
many cities.

00:12:15.870 --> 00:12:20.260
But there was one place in particular for
which Mansa Musa reserved most of his attention

00:12:20.260 --> 00:12:21.390
- Timbuktu.

00:12:21.390 --> 00:12:27.540
It’s somewhere you’ve almost certainly
heard of, even if you don’t know why and

00:12:27.540 --> 00:12:29.510
can’t point to it on a map.

00:12:29.510 --> 00:12:35.340
You may even think it’s some kind of mythical
location like Atlantis or El Dorado - don’t

00:12:35.340 --> 00:12:37.720
worry, plenty if people do.

00:12:37.720 --> 00:12:43.850
These days, Timbuktu is used in the English
language as a byword for somewhere unimaginably

00:12:43.850 --> 00:12:49.420
distant and remote, a status it likely earned
because no European managed to figure out

00:12:49.420 --> 00:12:51.880
how to get there until the 1830s.

00:12:51.880 --> 00:12:57.490
But the main reason the name became famous
in the first place is largely thanks to Mansa

00:12:57.490 --> 00:12:58.600
Musa.

00:12:58.600 --> 00:13:05.720
He annexed Timbuktu on his way back from Mecca
and set about turning it into one of the most

00:13:05.720 --> 00:13:12.740
important centres for trade, culture, religion,
and learning not just in Africa, but around

00:13:12.740 --> 00:13:14.840
the world.

00:13:14.840 --> 00:13:21.490
The Djinguereber Mosque, which Mansa Musa
paid for with 200 kilograms of solid gold,

00:13:21.490 --> 00:13:27.690
still stands today and makes up part of Timbuktu
University, for a time one of the world’s

00:13:27.690 --> 00:13:34.899
premiere seats of knowledge boasting 25,000
students and a library of a million books

00:13:34.899 --> 00:13:40.340
– that’s more than were held in the Great
Library of Alexandria at the height of its

00:13:40.340 --> 00:13:42.380
flame - I mean fame.

00:13:42.380 --> 00:13:49.190
And all this at a time when most Europeans
believed Africa was a continent full of uncultured

00:13:49.190 --> 00:13:50.460
savages.

00:13:50.460 --> 00:13:56.490
Putting an exact number on Mansa Musa’s
bank account today is obviously pretty difficult,

00:13:56.490 --> 00:14:03.310
but the figure most often cited - accounting
for inflation - is approximately 400 billion

00:14:03.310 --> 00:14:08.120
dollars – that’s about the GDP of Norway
or Ireland.

00:14:08.120 --> 00:14:12.430
In truth, it’s nothing more than a finger
in the air - not that it really matters.

00:14:12.430 --> 00:14:17.759
Mansa Musa was a man so wealthy that the exact
numbers involved aren’t important.

00:14:17.759 --> 00:14:20.880
His wealth was, to all intents and purposes,
limitless.

00:14:20.880 --> 00:14:25.450
He could do whatever he wanted, whenever he
felt like it.

00:14:25.450 --> 00:14:30.120
Luckily, it seems that what he most often
wanted to do was give his wealth away in order

00:14:30.120 --> 00:14:32.010
to improve the lives of others.

00:14:32.010 --> 00:14:52.950
And there are worse ways to spend the greatest
fortune in history, I’m sure you’ll agree.

00:14:52.950 --> 00:14:53.150
Thanks for watching.

